<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="flavonoid biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY1F-FLAVSYN" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; |FRAME: Flavonoids Flavonoids| are secondary metabolites formed from phenylpropanoid and fatty acid derivatives and have important functions, acting as UV-B protectors |CITS:[11724379]|, attractors of pollinators and seed dispersers, insect feeding attractors, anti-feedants, signal molecules in legume-rhizobium bacteria interactions |CITS:[3738520]|, and fungicides. Flavonoids have also received a lot of attention due to their effect of human health. In some plant species, flavonoids are required for fertility, but this is not the case in |FRAME:TAX-3701| |CITS:[8672888]|. |FRAME: Flavonoids Flavonoids| constitute a relatively diverse family of aromatic molecules that are derived from phenylalanine and malonyl-coenzyme A (via the fatty acid pathway) |CITS: [11402179]|. There are six major subgroups found in most higher plants: the |FRAME: Chalcones chalcones|, |FRAME: Flavones flavones|, |FRAME: Flavanols flavonols|, |FRAME: Flavonols flavanols|, |FRAME: Anthocyanins anthocyanins|, and |FRAME: PA-N condensed tannins| (proanthocyanidins); a seventh group, the |FRAME: AURONE aurones|, is not ubiquitous. |FRAME: Isoflavonoids Isoflavonoids| are found mostly in legumes but also in a small number of non-legume plants. Another specialized form of flavonoids, the phlobaphenes (monomers: 3-deoxyanthocyanins) have been detected in only a few species so far. Stilbenes, closely related to flavonoids, are synthesized by yet another group of unrelated species. Biosynthesis is thought to occur in the cytoplasm through enzymes that are associated with the endoplasmatic reticulum membranes |CITS:[3321063]| where the enzymes form a large, macromolecular complex |CITS:[10536025]|. Interestingly, the pathway also accommodates bifunctional enzymes that are involved in more than the presented flavonoid pathway above. For instance the naringenin 3-dioxygenases from &lt;i&gt;Citrus unshiu&lt;/i&gt; (CuFLS) |CITS:[12180990] [12620339]| and the gymnosperm &lt;i&gt;Ginkgo biloba&lt;/i&gt; (GbFLS1) |CITS:[21643949]| can also act as flavonol synthase in the |FRAME:PWY-3101|. In addition, the recombinant &lt;i&gt;Citrus&lt;/i&gt; naringenin 3-dioxygenase (FLS) had been shown to be able to accept the unusual substrate |FRAME:CPD-14042| to form the (-) isomer of |FRAME:DIHYDROKAEMPFEROL-CMPD|, i.e. |FRAME:CPD-15886| |CITS:[12620339]|. The structure of the major flavonoids in |FRAME:TAX-3701| have recently been described |CITS:[10514319]|. Under greenhouse conditions, only kaempferol glycosides accumulate in |FRAME:TAX-3701|. With higher UV exposure, quercetin metabolites can be detected as well |CITS:[10514319]|. Most flavonoids accumulate in the vacuole, the cuticular wax and in the cell wall |CITS:[10975502]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
  </Authors>
